Day 02 – Docker Images and Containers

Overview

On Day 2 of my Cloud and DevOps learning journey, I practised working with Docker images and containers using Docker Engine installed directly inside Ubuntu on WSL2.

1. Docker Image Management

- Downloaded the official Nginx image from Docker Hub using "docker pull nginx".
- Checked downloaded images using "docker images".
- Understood that Docker images are templates used to create containers.
- Learned that one image can be used to create multiple independent containers.

2. Running Containers

- Executed the "hello-world" image to verify that Docker was working correctly.
- Inspected containers using "docker ps" and "docker ps -a".
- Ran an Nginx container using port mapping:
  "docker run -d --name my-nginx -p 8080:80 nginx"
- Accessed the Nginx welcome page through "http://localhost:8080".

3. Container Lifecycle Management

Practised the following commands:

Command| Purpose
"docker pull nginx"| Download an image
"docker images"| List downloaded images
"docker run"| Create and start a container
"docker ps"| List running containers
"docker ps -a"| List all containers
"docker stop my-nginx"| Stop a container
"docker start my-nginx"| Start an existing container
"docker rm my-nginx"| Remove a container

4. Key Concepts Learned

- Difference between Docker images and containers.
- How Docker downloads images from Docker Hub.
- How containers are created and started from images.
- How to manage the container lifecycle.
- Host-to-container port mapping.
- How Nginx serves a web page from inside a Docker container.

Practical Outcome

Successfully downloaded the Nginx image, launched a container, accessed the Nginx welcome page through the browser, and practised stopping, restarting, and removing the container.

The Nginx image was retained after container removal for future use.

Environment

- Operating System: Ubuntu 26.04 LTS on WSL2
- Docker Engine: 29.1.3
- Web Server: Nginx
- Image Source: Docker Hub

